  "text": "To solve the problem, we start by letting \\( z = x + yi \\), where \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) are real numbers and \\( i \\) is the imaginary unit. We need to find the smallest possible value of \\( |z - 2i| \\) given that:\n\n\\[ |z^2 - 9| = |z(z - 3i)|. \\]\n\nFirst, we expand both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[ z^2 = (x + yi)^2 = x^2 + 2xyi + (yi)^2 = x^2 + 2xyi - y^2. \\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[ z^2 - 9 = x^2 + 2xyi - y^2 - 9 = (x^2 - y^2 - 9) + 2xyi. \\]\n\nThe magnitude of \\( z^2 - 9 \\) is:\n\n\\[ |z^2 - 9| = \\sqrt{(x^2 - y^2 - 9)^2 + (2xy)^2}. \\]\n\nNext, we expand \\( z(z - 3i) \\):\n\n\\[ z(z - 3i) = (x + yi)(x - 3iy) = x^2 + 3xyi - xyi - 3y^2i^2 = x^2 + 3y^2 + (3xy - xy)i = x^2 + 3y^2 + 2xyi. \\]\n\nThe magnitude of \\( z(z - 3i) \\) is:\n\n\\[ |z(z - 3i)| = \\sqrt{(x^2 + 3y^2)^2 + (2xy)^2}. \\]\n\nSetting these magnitudes equal to each other, we get:\n\n\\[ \\sqrt{(x^2 - y^2 - 9)^2 + (2xy)^2} = \\sqrt{(x^2 + 3y^2)^2 + (2xy)^2}. \\]\n\nSquaring both sides, we obtain:\n\n\\[ (x^2 - y^2 - 9)^2 + (2xy)^2 = (x^2 + 3y^2)^2 + (2xy)^2. \\]\n\nSubtracting \\( (2xy)^2 \\) from both sides, we get:\n\n\\[ (x^2 - y^2 - 9)^2 = (x^2 + 3y^2)^2. \\]\n\nSince the magnitudes are equal, we can consider the cases where the expressions inside the squares are equal in magnitude but opposite in sign. Thus, we have two cases:\n\n1. \\( x^2 - y^2 - 9 = x^2 + 3y^2 \\)\n2. \\( x^2 - y^2 - 9 = -(x^2 + 3y^2) \\)\n\nFor the first case:\n\n\\[ x^2 - y^2 - 9 = x^2 + 3y^2 \\implies -y^2 - 9 = 3y^2 \\implies -9 = 4y^2 \\implies y^2 = -\\frac{9}{4}. \\]\n\nThis is not possible since \\( y^2 \\) cannot be negative.\n\nFor the second case:\n\n\\[ x^2 - y^2 - 9 = -x^2 - 3y^2 \\implies x^2 + 3y^2 - y^2 - 9 = 0 \\implies x^2 + 2y^2 = 9. \\]\n\nSo, we have the equation \\( x^2 + 2y^2 = 9 \\). We need to minimize \\( |z - 2i| = \\sqrt{x^2 + (y-2)^2} \\). To do this, we can express \\( x^2 \\) in terms of \\( y \\):\n\n\\[ x^2 = 9 - 2y^2. \\]\n\nThen,\n\n\\[ |z - 2i| = \\sqrt{x^2 + (y-2)^2} = \\sqrt{9 - 2y^2 + (y-2)^2} = \\sqrt{9 - 2y^2 + y^2 - 4y + 4} = \\sqrt{13 - y^2 - 4y}. \\]\n\nTo minimize this expression, we need to find the value of \\( y \\) that minimizes \\( 13 - y^2 - 4y \\). Completing the square, we get:\n\n\\[ 13 - y^2 - 4y = 13 - (y^2 + 4y) = 13 - (y^2 + 4y + 4 - 4) = 13 - (y+2)^2 + 4 = 17 - (y+2)^2. \\]\n\nThe minimum value of \\( (y+2)^2 \\) is 0, which occurs when \\( y = -2 \\). Substituting \\( y = -2 \\) into the expression for \\( |z - 2i| \\), we get:\n\n\\[ |z - 2i| = \\sqrt{17 - 0} = \\sqrt{17}. \\]\n\nThus, the smallest possible value of \\( |z - 2i| \\) is \\( \\boxed{\\sqrt{17}} \\).",
